ely, maybe I should take the center? No, it's already taken by X. \n\nAnother approach is to check for possible winning moves. If I can place O in a position that creates a line, that's good. But currently, there's no line for O. \n\nSo possible moves: Let's think about where X might play next. If X is trying to win, they might take a corner or edge. For example, if X takes (0,0), then they can complete a diagonal with the center. So I need to block that. So if I place O in (0,0), that would block X from that diagonal. Similarly, if X takes (2,2), same thing. \n\nAlternatively, if X takes (0,1) or (1,0), etc., but I don't know. \n\nSince the current board is:\n\n_ _ _\n_ X _\n_ _ _\n\nIf I place O in (0,0), then the board becomes:\n\nO _ _\n_ X _\n_ _ _\n\nThis blocks X from completing the diagonal. Alternatively, placing O in (2,2) would do the same. \n\nAlternatively, maybe I should take the center? No, it's already taken. \n\nAlternatively, maybe take (0,1) or (1,0) to block X's possible lines. \n\nBut since X is the other player, and they have just played the center, their next move might be to take a corner. So I should block that. \n\nSo the best move is to take either (0,0) or (2,2) to block the diagonal. \n\nAlternatively, maybe take (0,1) or (1,0) to block other lines. \n\nBut since the center is already taken, maybe the best is to block the diagonal. Let me choose (0,0) as my move. \n\nSo the action would be O(0,0). \n\nAlternatively, maybe (2,2) is better. But either way, blocking the diagonal. \n\nAlternatively, maybe taking (0,1) to block a vertical line? But X is in the center. If X takes (0,1), then they have a vertical line with the center? No, that's not possible. \n\nWait, X is in (1,1). If X takes (0,1), then they have a vertical line? No, because (1,1) is center, (0,1) is top middle. To make a vertical line, they need (0,1), (1,1), (2,1). So if X takes (0,1), then they have two in a column. But that's not a win yet. \n\nBut maybe I should block that. However, since X is the other player, they might not be trying to make that line. \n\nSo perhaps the best move is to block the diagonal. So I'll choose O(0,0) or O(2,2). \n\nBut which one? Well, maybe either is fine. Let's pick O(0,0). \n\nSo the answer is <answer>O(0,0)</answer>.\n</think>\n\n<answer>O(0,0)</answer><|im_end|>"}, {"prompt": "system\nYou are an AI agent that makes optimal decisions to win in the game of tic-tac-toe.user\nGAME RULES:\n1. Tic-tac-toe is a two-player board game played on a three-by-three grid. The grid is 0-indexed, where (0,0) is the top-left corner and (2,2) is the bottom-right corner.\n2. Two players take turns placing their marks X and O in empty cells of the grid.\n3. The player who first places three of their marks in a horizontal, vertical, or diagonal line wins.\n4. If all cells are filled and no player wins, the game ends in a draw.\n\nPLAYER INFORMATION:\n1. Your mark is X. You are competing with another player controlling the mark O.\n2. In each of your turns:\n   a. The game state demonstrates the current board with a three-line text grid, where 'X' and 'O' are the marks of the two players, and '_' represents empty cells.\n   b. You need to chose an action to place your mark in an empty cell, based on the given game state and the history of your decisions.\n   c. All legal actions for the current turn are provided in the format of `<X({row},{column})>`, where `X` is your mark, and {row} and {column} are integers indicating the row and column of the cell to place your mark.\n\nRESPONSE INSTRUCTIONS:\nAlways choose only one action from the legal actions and output `<answer>{your chosen action}</answer>` with no extra text after you finish the thinking process. For example, `<answer><X(0,0)></answer>`. Strictly follow the above format and keep your thinking process concise. Responses that do not follow the format will result in immediate loss of the game.\n\nInformation of Turn-1:\n\nThis is your turn. The game state and l