[2026-09-24 01:13:16] [agentic_pipeline.py (367)] [INFO] [DRIVER 0 / 1][PID 4413] [{"prompt": "system\nYou are an AI agent that makes optimal decisions to win in the game of tic-tac-toe.user\nGAME RULES:\n1. Tic-tac-toe is a two-player board game played on a three-by-three grid. The grid is 0-indexed, where (0,0) is the top-left corner and (2,2) is the bottom-right corner.\n2. Two players take turns placing their marks X and O in empty cells of the grid.\n3. The player who first places three of their marks in a horizontal, vertical, or diagonal line wins.\n4. If all cells are filled and no player wins, the game ends in a draw.\n\nPLAYER INFORMATION:\n1. Your mark is X. You are competing with another player controlling the mark O.\n2. In each of your turns:\n   a. The game state demonstrates the current board with a three-line text grid, where 'X' and 'O' are the marks of the two players, and '_' represents empty cells.\n   b. You need to chose an action to place your mark in an empty cell, based on the given game state and the history of your decisions.\n   c. All legal actions for the current turn are provided in the format of `<X({row},{column})>`, where `X` is your mark, and {row} and {column} are integers indicating the row and column of the cell to place your mark.\n\nRESPONSE INSTRUCTIONS:\nAlways choose only one action from the legal actions and output `<answer>{your chosen action}</answer>` with no extra text after you finish the thinking process. For example, `<answer><X(0,0)></answer>`. Strictly follow the above format and keep your thinking process concise. Responses that do not follow the format will result in immediate loss of the game.\n\nInformation of Turn-1:\n\nThis is your turn. The game state and legal actions for this turn are provided below. Please choose your action and strictly follow the given output format in the response instructions.\n\nGAME STATE:\n___\n___\n___\n\nLEGAL ACTIONS:\n<X(0,0)>, <X(0,1)>, <X(0,2)>, <X(1,0)>, <X(1,1)>, <X(1,2)>, <X(2,0)>, <X(2,1)>, <X(2,2)>.\n\nassistant\n", "response": "<think>\nOkay, so I need to play tic-tac-toe as X. Let me think about the best first move. Since the board is completely empty, I should aim to take the center. Wait, but maybe the center is a good position because it's part of multiple lines. But wait, in the first move, maybe the best is to take the center. Wait, but the center is (1,1). Let me check the possible moves. The legal actions are all the cells. So, I can choose any. But what's the optimal strategy here? In tic-tac-toe, the center is usually a good move because it gives more opportunities to create lines. So I should go for the center. So the action would be X(1,1). That makes sense. Let me confirm. Yes, starting in the center is a strong move. So I'll choose that.\n</think>\n\n<answer><X(1,1)></answer>user\nInformation of Turn-2:\n\nThis is the other player's turn. The game state, legal actions, as well as the chosen action of the other player for this turn are provided below.\n\nGAME STATE:\n___\n_X_\n___\n\nLEGAL ACTIONS:\n<O(0,0)>, <O(0,1)>, <O(0,2)>, <O(1,0)>, <O(1,2)>, <O(2,0)>, <O(2,1)>, <O(2,2)>.\n\n", "episode_score": 0.05, "llm_raw_text": "<|im_start|>assistant\n<think>\nOkay, so I need to play tic-tac-toe as X. Let me think about the best first move. Since the board is completely empty, I should aim to take the center. Wait, but maybe the center is a good position because it's part of multiple lines. But wait, in the first move, maybe the best is to take the center. Wait, but the center is (1,1). Let me check the possible moves. The legal actions are all the cells. So, I can choose any. But what's the optimal strategy here? In tic-tac-toe, the center is usually a good move because it gives more opportunities to create lines. So I should go for the center. So the action would be X(1,1). That makes sense. Let me confirm. Yes, starting in the center is a strong move. So I'll choose that.\n</think>\n\n<answer><X(1,1)></answer><|im_end|><|im_start|>user\nInformation of Turn-2:\n\nThis is the other player's turn. The game state, legal actions, as well as the chosen action of the other player for this turn are provided below.\n\nGAME STATE:\n___\n_X_\n___\n\nLEGAL ACTIONS:\n<O(0,0)>, <O(0,1)>, <O(0,2)>, <O(1,0)>, <O(1,2)>, <O(2,0)>, <O(2,1)>, <O(2,2)>.\n\n<|im_end|>"}, {"prompt": "system\nYou are an AI agent that makes optimal decisions to win in the game of tic-tac-toe.user\nGAME RULES:\n1. Tic-tac-toe is a two-player board game played on a three-by-three grid. The grid is 0-indexed, where (0,0) is the top-left corner and (2,2) is the bottom-right corner.\n2. Two players take turns placing their marks X and O in empty cells of the grid.\n3. The player who first places three of their marks in a horizontal, vertical, or diagonal line wins.\n4. If all cells are filled and no player wins, the game ends in a draw.\n\nPLAYER INFORMATION:\n1. Your mark is O. You are competing with another player controlling the mark X.\n2. In each of your turns:\n   a. The game state demonstrates the current board with a three-line text grid, where 'X' and 'O' are the marks of the two players, and '_' represents empty cells.\n   b. You need to chose an action to place your mark in an empty cell, based on the given game state and the history of your decisions.\n   c. All legal actions for the current turn are provided in the format of `<O({row},{column})>`, where `O` is your mark, and {row} and {column} are integers indicating the row and column of the cell to place your mark.\n\nRESPONSE INSTRUCTIONS:\nAlways choose only one action from the legal actions and output `<answer>{your chosen action}</answer>` with no extra text after you finish the thinking process. For example, `<answer><O(0,0)></answer>`. Strictly follow the above format and keep your thinking process concise. Responses that do not follow the format will result in immediate loss of the game.\n\nInformation of Turn-1:\n\nThis is the other player's turn. The game state, legal actions, as well as the chosen action of the other player for this turn are provided below.\n\nGAME STATE:\n___\n___\n___\n\nLEGAL ACTIONS:\n<X(0,0)>, <X(0,1)>, <X(0,2)>, <X(1,0)>, <X(1,1)>, <X(1,2)>, <X(2,0)>, <X(2,1)>, <X(2,2)>.\n\nCHOSEN ACTION:\n<X(1,1)>\nInformation of Turn-2:\n\nThis is your turn. The game state and legal actions for this turn are provided below. Please choose your action and strictly follow the given output format in the response instructions.\n\nGAME STATE:\n___\n_X_\n___\n\nLEGAL ACTIONS:\n<O(0,0)>, <O(0,1)>, <O(0,2)>, <O(1,0)>, <O(1,2)>, <O(2,0)>, <O(2,1)>, <O(2,2)>.\n\nassistant\n", "response": "<think>\nOkay, let's see. The game is in Turn 2, and it's my turn as player O. The current game state is:\n\n___\n_X_\n___\n\nSo the board has the middle cell (1,1) already occupied by X from the previous turn. The other cells are empty. The legal actions for me are to place an O in any of the remaining cells except (1,1). \n\nI need to choose a move that's optimal. Since the opponent is X, and they went for the center in the first move, maybe they're trying to create a winning line. But since it's my turn, I should block any potential threats and try to set up my own.\n\nLooking at the board, the X is in the center. If X continues to play in the center, they might try to form a line. But since the board is mostly empty, maybe I should take a position that can block X from completing a line. Alternatively, I can try to create my own potential lines.\n\nThe possible moves are all cells except (1,1). Let's think about where to place O. \n\nIf I place O in (0,1), that's the top middle. That could help block X from making a diagonal. Or maybe (2,1) for the bottom middle. Alternatively, (0,0) or (0,2) to take corners. \n\nWait, the opponent's first move was center, so maybe they're aiming for a corner? But in the current state, the X is in the center. Let me think about possible threats. \n\nIf X is in the center, they could try to make a line by taking adjacent cells. For example, if X takes (0,1), (1,0), (2,2), but that's not possible yet. Alternatively, maybe X is trying to create a line vertically or horizontally. \n\nBut since it's my turn, I should block any immediate threats. Let's see: the X is in the center. If X takes a corner, they can form a line. For example, if X takes (0,0), then they could have a line from (0,0), (1,1), (2,2). But since X is already in the center, they just need to take one more corner. \n\nSo I should prevent that. Therefore, maybe I should take one of the corners to block X from completing that diagonal. Alternatively, take a side cell to block a possible line. \n\nAlternatively, maybe I should take the center? But the center is already taken by X. So no. \n\nLooking at the possible moves, the best options are corners or edges. Let me consider the possible moves. \n\nIf I take (0,0), that would block X from completing the diagonal. Similarly, taking (2,2) would block the same. Alternatively, taking (0,2) or (2,0). \n\nAlternatively, if I take (0,1), that's the top middle. That could help block X from making a horizontal line if they take (0,0) and (0,2). \n\nBut since X is in the center, maybe they are trying to make a line. Let me think: X is in the center. If X takes (0,1), then they can have a vertical line. But that's not possible yet. \n\nAlternatively, if I take (1,0), that would be the middle left. That could block X from making a vertical line if they take (0,0) and (2,0). \n\nBut since the game is in the early stages, maybe I should focus on taking a corner. Let's say I take (0,0). Then, if X takes (0,1), they can't make a line. \n\nAlternatively, taking (0,0) would be a good move to block the diagonal. So, I think the best move here is to take a corner. Let's choose (0,0). \n\nAlternatively, maybe (2,2) is better. But either way, blocking the diagonal. So I'll go with (0,0). \n\nWait, but the legal actions include O(0,0), so that's allowed. So the answ