ces in (1,2) and (0,2), they'd have a column. But since X is already at (2,2), if I block (1,2), then X can't get two in column 2. But maybe that's not as critical as blocking the row.\n\nAlternatively, maybe I should look for creating my own opportunities. For example, if I place O in (1,1), the center, that could be a good position for future moves. But I need to check if that's a good idea.\n\nBut considering that the opponent is X, and they just placed at (2,2), maybe they are trying to get a row or column. So blocking the third row is important. Let me check the third row again. The third row has (2,2) as X. So if X places in (2,0) and (2,1), they win. So I should block that. The best way to block is to place O in (2,1) or (2,0). But since the opponent is X, they might go for (2,0) next. Alternatively, if I place O in (2,1), then they can't have two in that row. So that's a good move.\n\nAlternatively, if I place O in (2,0), then X can't get two in that row. But then, maybe the opponent would go for (2,1) next. So either way, blocking the third row is important.\n\nAnother option is to block the column 2. If X places in (1,2) and (0,2), they'd win. So if I place O in (1,2), that would block that. But maybe that's not as critical as blocking the row.\n\nAlternatively, maybe the opponent is trying to make a diagonal. If they have X in (2,2) and (1,1), then they could win with (0,0). But currently, there's no X in (1,1) or (0,0). So that's not a threat.\n\nSo the best move is to block the third row. Let's choose (2,1) to block the third row. That way, if X tries to complete the row, they can't. So I'll place O at (2,1). That's a good move.\n\nAlternatively, maybe I should look for a center move. But the center is (1,1). If I place O there, that could help in future moves. But in this case, the immediate threat is the third row. So blocking that is more urgent.\n\nSo I think the best move is to place O at (2,1). That would be <O(2,1)>.\n</think>\n\n<O(2,1)><|im_end